WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 10.4 · Legal, Compliance, Policies & Risk
Core policies: conduct, conflicts and anti-fraud

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Write a few short core policies before habits form, and share them from the first volunteer.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:26.281
Core policies are the small set most organisations need: conduct, conflicts of interest, anti-fraud and anti-harassment. They are easiest to write before habits form, and they apply from your first volunteer.

4
00:00:26.681 --> 00:00:27.281
The core set

5
00:00:27.281 --> 00:00:38.241
Code of conduct: The behaviour expected of everyone: respect, boundaries, care with resources and reporting concerns. For example: Nobody takes unsold bread home without logging it on the waste sheet.

6
00:00:38.241 --> 00:00:50.311
Conflicts of interest: People declare personal interests that could sway a decision, and each conflict is recorded. For example: A founder declares her cousin supplies flour and stays out of choosing the supplier.

7
00:00:50.311 --> 00:01:01.270
Anti-fraud and bribery: Fraud, bribes and payments to speed things up are never accepted. For example: The bakery refuses to pay an unofficial fee to speed up its market-stall permit.

8
00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:14.911
Anti-harassment: Protects people from harassment and bullying, and gives them a route for complaints. For example: A trainee can report unwanted comments to a named person she trusts at the youth centre.

9
00:01:15.311 --> 00:01:15.911
Why write them early

10
00:01:15.911 --> 00:01:21.956
1. Friends and family often help at the start, so conflicts are common.

11
00:01:21.956 --> 00:01:26.148
2. Clear rules protect people before problems arise.

12
00:01:26.148 --> 00:01:31.711
3. Funders and partners often require them by contract.

13
00:01:32.111 --> 00:01:32.711
How you put them in place

14
00:01:32.711 --> 00:01:39.496
1. Share each one with everyone who joins and ask them to confirm reading it.

15
00:01:39.496 --> 00:01:44.059
2. Keep a register of declared interests and gifts.

16
00:01:44.059 --> 00:01:51.104
3. List each in your policy register with an owner and review date.

17
00:01:51.504 --> 00:01:52.104
What to avoid

18
00:01:52.104 --> 00:01:55.926
Long, legal-sounding policies that nobody reads.

19
00:01:55.926 --> 00:02:00.859
Waiting until a problem happens to write the rules.

20
00:02:00.859 --> 00:02:07.904
Forgetting to say how to report a concern and what happens next.

21
00:02:08.504 --> 00:02:10.304
You outline your core policies and how each will work from the start.

22
00:02:10.304 --> 00:02:14.304
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:15.804 --> 00:02:18.904
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
